  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Latest numerical model guidance\ncontinues to show a ULT over the West Coast. The trough will \ndeepen overnight and a cutoff low will form over OR and meander \nsouth into northern CA by Wednesday. With the low directly west of\nthe CWA a tighter pressure gradient in its downstream flow will \nenhance southwesterly flow at the surface. This will create \nelevated fire weather conditions through Thursday afternoon. \nSustained southwesterly winds of 20-25 mph and gusts of 30-35 mph \nare possible. Patchy blowing dust across central NV is expected \nduring the afternoon hours both Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nLimited moisture and sufficient forcing associated with the low\nwill create light shower activity along the border especially in\nnorthern Humboldt County on Thursday. Subtropical moisture \nstreaming north across UT will spill over in extreme eastern NV\nTuesday evening and overnight into Wednesday. Sufficient\ninstability and LI values of 0 to -2 over eastern White Pine\nCounty create low probabilities of mainly dry thunderstorm \nactivity tonight into Wednesday morning. Rain accumulation \nassociated with this activity is limited: trace to several \nhundredths mainly in elevated regions of eastern White Pine \nCounty. \n\nThe cutoff low slowly meanders eastward north of the state and\nintroduces brief ridging over the SW CONUS by the weekend. This\nwill initiate a warming trend late week that will bring afternoon\nhigh temperatures back into the upper 70s and low 80s. The weekend\nis expected to be dry under the ridge as another shortwave\nstrengthens to the northwest off the west coast by Monday.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence remains in\nelevated fire weather conditions Tuesday through Thursday\nafternoon. Low confidence exists for showers along the northern NV\nborder and in eastern White Pine County this week. Moderate\nconfidence remains in a warming trend into the weekend. Major grid\nedits were not necessary at this time.",
